# Node.js + TypeScript

## Language & runtime
- Prefer TypeScript over plain JavaScript for new files
- Use ES modules (`import`/`export`); avoid CommonJS `require` unless a dependency forces it
- Target modern Node (LTS); use built-in APIs before adding packages

## Types
- Prefer explicit types on public APIs and function returns; let inference work for locals
- Avoid `any`; use `unknown` and narrow, or define a proper type/interface
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes; use `type` for unions, intersections, and mapped types

## Async & errors
- Prefer `async`/`await` over raw Promise chains
- Never swallow errors with empty `catch`; log or rethrow with context
- Propagate typed/domain errors from services; map to HTTP status only at the route layer

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
try {
  await loadUser(id);
} catch (e) {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  await loadUser(id);
} catch (e) {
  throw new Error(`Failed to load user ${id}`, { cause: e });
}
```

## Project structure
- Keep route/handler thin; put business logic in services/modules
- Colocate types next to the code that owns them
- Use named exports; default export only when the module has a single clear primary export

## Style
- Match existing formatting; do not invent new patterns mid-file
- No drive-by refactors or unrelated file edits
- Prefer small, focused modules over large utility dumps
